  "result": "Implemented `catalog/service.py`. All 4 tests pass.\n\n**What's in the package**\n\n- `NotFound` exception and a `Catalog` class backed by an insertion-ordered dict, so `list()` returns records in creation order for free.\n- `get`, `update`, and `list` return copies of the stored dicts so callers can't mutate internal state.\n- `update` validates field names before touching the record and raises `ValueError` listing the offending fields. Unknown-field checks happen before the existence check, so a bad field on a missing id reports the more actionable error.\n- `delete` on a missing id raises `NotFound`, and deleted ids are never reused.\n\n**Approach chosen:** Approach A, sequential integers. The spec is a single in-memory, per-process catalog with no distributed generation or client-side id creation, so UUIDs would buy nothing here while sequential ids are shorter, sortable, and easier to read in tests and logs.",
